# Exchange Rates and Balance of Payments: India in the World Economy

> When the rupee "falls" against the dollar, petrol prices climb,
> imported laptops cost more, and software exporters cheer. What is
> actually moving is a price — the price of one currency in terms of
> another — set by a market that never sleeps. This lesson decodes
> that market: how exchange rates are quoted, why the balance of
> payments must always balance, and what depreciation really does to
> an economy.

## 1. The Balance of Payments: A Country's Ledger

[definition]
The **Balance of Payments (BoP)** is a systematic record of all
economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of
the world during a year. By accounting construction it always
balances — every surplus somewhere is matched by a deficit
elsewhere.
[/definition]

| Account | Records | Typical entries (India) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Current account** | Trade in goods & services, income, transfers | Merchandise exports/imports, software exports, remittances (largest inflow), investment income |
| **Capital account** | Transfers of assets, non-produced assets | Debt forgiveness, migrants' transfers |
| **Financial account** (often clubbed with capital) | FDI, FPI, loans, banking capital | Foreign investment inflows, NRI deposits, external commercial borrowing |
| **Errors & omissions** | Statistical discrepancy | Balancing residual |

**Autonomous items** are transactions undertaken for profit or need
(exporters, investors); **accommodating items** (official reserve
transactions by the RBI) fill the residual gap. A current account
deficit is not automatically a disease — it means the country is a
net borrower from the world, which is fine while lenders believe in
future returns.

## 2. The Foreign Exchange Market and Quotations

The rupee-dollar rate is the price that equilibrates demand and
supply of foreign exchange: importers, tourists, and outbound
investors demand dollars; exporters, inward remittances, and foreign
investors supply them.

- **Depreciation:** market-driven fall of the home currency (₹68 →
  ₹76 per $) — more rupees per dollar.
- **Appreciation:** market-driven rise.
- **Devaluation/revaluation:** deliberate official changes under a
  **fixed or managed exchange rate** regime.

[important]
Depreciation makes exports cheaper to foreigners and imports costlier
to residents — trade theory says it should improve the current
account. But two lags blunt the effect: contracts and supply chains
reprice slowly (the **J-curve**), and if exports are import-heavy
(oil, machinery), costlier inputs claw back much of the gain.
[/important]

## 3. Exchange Rate Regimes

| Regime | Mechanism | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fixed (pegged) | Central bank defends a set rate, buying/selling reserves | Classic gold standard; Hong Kong's currency board |
| Floating (flexible) | Market sets the rate; reserves untouched | US, Japan, UK |
| Managed floating (dirty float) | Market-led, with central bank intervening to smooth swings | **India since 1993** |

India moved from a fixed rupee to a **market-determined (managed
float)** exchange rate as part of the 1991 reforms; the RBI intervenes
only to curb excessive volatility, not to defend any specific level.

## 4. What Moves the Rupee

- **Trade and oil:** India imports about 85 percent of its crude — a
  $10/bbl rise widens the current account and pressures the rupee.
- **Inflation differential:** persistently higher domestic inflation
  erodes competitiveness and the currency over the long run
  (purchasing-power logic).
- **Interest-rate gaps:** higher Indian rates attract portfolio
  ("hot") money, supporting the rupee — until sentiment flips.
- **Foreign investment cycles:** FII equity outflows (taper tantrum
  2013, pandemic 2020) drain dollar supply sharply.
- **RBI policy:** interventions, interest rates, and the signalling
  power of reserves (India's war chest has crossed $600 billion).
- **Global shocks:** the Ukraine war's energy spike and US Federal
  Reserve tightening both pushed the rupee to successive lows — a
  global dollar tide no single country swims against.

## 5. Convertibility and the SDR

The rupee is **fully convertible on the current account** — you may
freely buy forex for trade, education, travel within limits — but
only **partially convertible on the capital account**: portfolio
and loan flows face quantity and price restrictions, a deliberate
guard against sudden exits learned from the 1997 Asian crisis.

The IMF's **Special Drawing Right (SDR)** is a basket reserve asset
(USD, EUR, CNY, JPY, GBP) in which the rupee's weight is zero;
India's quota and SDR holdings form part of its reserves. The SDR is
neither a currency nor a claim on the IMF itself, and its valuation
is reviewed every five years.

## 6. Winners, Losers, and Policy

| Rupee depreciates | Rupee appreciates |
| --- | --- |
| Exporters (IT, pharma, textiles) gain | Importers gain |
| Importers (oil, electronics) and travellers lose | Exporters' margins compress |
| NRI remittances rise in rupee terms | Foreign education, foreign travel cheaper |
| Imported inflation (fuel → everything) | Disinflationary pressure |
| External debt service (in $ terms) costs more rupees | Debt burden eases |

Policy responses span the spectrum: intervention in the foreign
exchange market, interest rate defence, encouraging NRI deposits and
foreign borrowing, curbing non-essential imports, and — the durable
cure — raising export competitiveness and domestic manufacturing
(plug-and-play schemes, PLI, trade agreements).

[example]
Consider two identical 2013 and 2022 episodes: talk of US Fed
tapering triggers FPI outflows, dollar demand spikes, and the rupee
slides. In 2013 (thin reserves, wide CAD near 4.8 percent of GDP),
the slide became a rout — the RBI had to raise rates and attract
FCNR(B) deposits. In 2022 (reserves above $600 billion, CAD under
control), the same global shock produced a far gentler, managed
decline. Reserves and credibility are the shock absorbers of a
floating-currency economy.
[/example]

[summary]
The balance of payments records every transaction with the world —
current account (goods, services, income, remittances), capital and
financial accounts (FDI, FPI, loans) — and always balances, with
RBI reserve movements as the accommodating plug. The exchange rate
is a price: depreciation (market-driven) cheapens exports and
dearers imports with a J-curve lag, while devaluation is an
official act under pegs; India runs a managed float since 1993.
Rupee fortunes swing on oil, inflation gaps, interest differentials,
FPI tides, and RBI defences; the rupee is fully convertible on the
current account but only partially on the capital account. Reserves,
SDR holdings, and export competitiveness — not the rate itself —
are what determine how a country weathers global storms.
[/summary]
